plan CommandLine
title   Discover, run and inspect apps through flow
status  proposed
goal
    App authors discover and run Stories, inspect declarations and explain retained evidence
    through the installed flow binary without changing the application being inspected.
outcomes
    O1  Commands and selection
        flow exposes only story list, story describe <story-id>, story run <story-id>, trace
        <story-id> [--at <checkpoint>], graph [<app-id/machine-id>], check and explain (the
        user's CLI scope, OD55); there is no diff, trace diff or search command. All support text
        and JSON output; graph also supports Mermaid. Text is the default. Help and version are
        conventional text. Output for people names source locations (pay.machine.ts:42), never
        internal closure paths, prints a trace that reads like the Story rather than evidence
        sequence numbers, and groups a graph by state; the exact outputs are to be accepted by the
        user in phase 5.

        Source commands accept --project-root and --app. --app selects an exact app id. Without
        it, list lists all Stories and graph/check inspect all apps; commands selecting a Story or
        machine require a unique exact match. Ambiguous machine-id prefixes require --app; an
        explicit app must agree with the operand. Unknown apps or operands fail rather than
        inspecting another app.

        story list returns declared identities; story list --coverage (OD55) lists the transitions,
        exits, callbacks and ending handlers (current and late), write-lane cells, stream cells and
        Flow reasons no Story reaches, stated as reached, never as checked. story describe prints,
        from the plan alone, the subject and its kind, options, every step with its index in its
        data form, checkpoints and every fake server the plan names with its endpoints, controls
        and their Schemas; it has no expectation or property lines. graph prints one edge per
        alternative, named by its place in the source so an edge and the Runtime's turn record
        share one name; each edge says what decides it (a state token, an idle or retryable lane
        fact, a port, a closure) and is marked late, shadowed, unreached or unsourced when the data
        says it cannot be taken; fillings, schedule triggers and whenAbandoned callbacks are
        edges; what data cannot say is listed as unseen (@plan.Inspection.O6 renders it). explain
        accepts a machine, state path and public event, or a domain with an optional --read.
        Static explanations show possible transitions, denials and fallthrough without evaluating
        guards, each commit's operation, contract, verb, policy and lane key with its cardinality
        (and subject for a fragment operation), each stream placement (kind, session per key,
        fills, idle time, reconnect, catch-up), each schedule's path to a write, a listen entry's
        covers terms, and for a commit to a domain write its predicted entity, key and field
        names, the reads they can repair, their carriers and the placements that subscribe them;
        or domain reads and their impact. explain --trace <artifact|-> --at <checkpoint> uses
        recorded evidence only and says "evidence unavailable" for what fell outside the window.
        explain <release> --since <release> (proposed spelling) compares two compiled releases and
        says per definition: its saved state resets, records saved in a removed state reset, it
        restores, or its identity fields changed (old-version records, kept, never reset); writes
        in flight are always kept. It compares two releases, not the chain a record went through.

        Invalid, missing, repeated or conflicting arguments fail before application loading.
        Selectors are data and never execute expressions. Artifact-only commands accept no source
        selection options and load no application or config. At most one input may be stdin (-).

        Superseded: "trace summarize <artifact|->" is replaced by trace <story-id> [--at] (C141).
        "trace diff <left|-> <right|->" and "diff --base <git-revision>", with its revision-diff
        sentences, are cut by the user's CLI scope of 2026-11-02 (OD55); release comparison is
        explain --since (C216). "story list returns declared identities; describe returns actors,
        commands and checkpoints" is replaced by describe's plan-only listing and coverage (C81;
        T2). "graph uses @plan.Inspection.O6" gains the edge rules (C141). The domain-write
        explain lines are added (merge 5, 6.4; C18; C101; C125).
    O2  Execution and safe trace output
        Only story run and trace start application work. Discovery, graph, check and static
        explain may load declarations, including lazy entries, but activate no actors, services,
        fixtures or machine callbacks; they read each machine's compiled data view. Running a
        Story through flow produces the same checkpoints, endings and failure evidence as running
        it directly under @plan.Testing.O1. story run reports what ran, with no pass or fail on
        correctness: steps, checkpoints with their state and sequence, endings reached, requests
        per endpoint, issue records and UnusedWireRule diagnostics, with the earliest error as
        primary; it exits 2 when the run rejected, printing the FlowError's boundary and evidence.
        trace runs the Story once and prints its timeline: each step in its data form, then the
        Runtime's evidence in that step (turns with the alternative each part took, endings,
        mints, lane cells), the wire's phase changes, and checkpoints with their sequence; --at
        stops there. Cancellation and cleanup evidence follow @plan.Testing.O8.

        Artifact inputs are validated under @plan.Inspection.O4, including bounds while reading
        stdin. Invalid or incompatible artifacts fail before a query reports an answer. story run
        --trace-output <path> checks destination usability before starting the Story. Existing
        entries reject unless --overwrite is supplied; --overwrite without a destination rejects.
        Replacement of a symlink changes the link entry, never its target. Publication is atomic:
        readers see a complete artifact; pre-publication failure leaves an existing destination
        unchanged. A racing creator is never overwritten without consent. A published artifact is
        never rolled back. Temporary files are readable only by the invoking user and cleaned on
        every exit, and cleanup failures are reported. Unsupported safe publication fails without
        truncation.

        Requested trace output is attempted even after execution failure; the execution failure
        remains primary and drain, encoding, write and cleanup failures remain visible. A Story run
        never yields to the host between its first step and its cleanup, so no handler can run
        mid-run and none is installed; an interruption is handled before the run starts or after
        it ends, waits for bounded disposal under @plan.Runtime.O6, and attempts requested partial
        trace output before reporting interruption. Partial traces retain accepted evidence and
        cleanup truth and never fabricate an end. Cleanup or trace-output failure cannot replace
        interruption as the primary result. Forced process termination provides no cleanup
        guarantee.

        Superseded: "Only story run starts application work." adds trace (C141). "Interruption
        stops later Story commands" is replaced: no mid-run handler is installed, since a run
        never yields (C70; C75; 2.10.14). story run's report and exit are added (C81).
    O3  Results for people and automation
        Text and JSON describe the same result. JSON supplies version: 1, the command leaf,
        status, data and ordered diagnostics; absent data is null. Status is completed, equal,
        different, incomplete or failed. Diagnostic values follow @plan.Diagnostics.O2; execution
        failures use FlowError under @plan.Diagnostics.O1.

        Successful invocations and comparison/check findings emit one newline-terminated document
        on stdout. Invocation failures emit one document on stderr. Results never mix with loader
        console output or include ANSI in JSON/non-interactive text. Exported sensitive values
        follow @plan.Inspection.O5; raw causes, stacks and private adapter authority never appear.
        A broken output pipe fails without recursively attempting to print another error.

        Completed commands, warning-only checks, equal comparisons and explanations/summaries with
        insufficient evidence exit 0. Differences, incomplete trace comparisons and checks with
        errors exit 1 and remain stdout results. Usage, loading, artifact, Story execution,
        cleanup and I/O failures exit 2. Interruption reports failure only after cleanup and exits
        130 for SIGINT or 143 for SIGTERM; a second signal may end the process at once, without
        cleanup. Product failures recorded as Story issues do not alone fail the invocation; a
        completed Story has an end and no execution failure. Cleanup failure cannot report
        success.
    O4  Trace queries
        Queries over an exported trace artifact come with that export (@plan.Inspection.O4). A
        summary reports counts and an ordered timeline over the retained evidence. --actor
        selects an exact actor and --attempt an attempt id; --view issues selects issues rather
        than the timeline. --read <domain.entry> reports repairs and their causes; --entity
        <Entity:key> reports field sources, candidates, predictions, uncertainty and queued work.
        Entity keys split at the first colon, preserving later colons. --sequence selects a
        nonnegative safe-integer cut for entity queries only. No selector means the whole retained
        timeline.

        Evidence explain uses the selected checkpoint to report recorded eligibility, lane,
        ending, store, session or domain repair evidence without executing current code or
        inventing reasons for unconsidered candidates. An absent selector/checkpoint in a
        truncated window is insufficient evidence, not proof of absence. Unknown selectors in
        complete evidence fail with InvalidArtifactOperand (reason UnknownSelector). Answers
        include the selection and completeness marker. Projection and comparison semantics follow
        @plan.Inspection.O6. Comparing two traces is the library's projection
        (@plan.Inspection.O6); the CLI has no command for it.

        Superseded: "trace summarize reports counts and an ordered timeline" becomes the artifact
        summary, and trace <story-id> prints a Story's timeline (C141). The paragraph "trace diff
        optionally selects sections with --section, from the closed section list of
        @plan.Inspection.O6; omission compares all sections. Equal requires complete matching
        evidence. Differing retained facts prove difference; matching truncated evidence is
        incomplete and never proves equality. Both completeness markers accompany the result.
        Incompatible identities fail rather than report difference. Independently redacted values
        never establish equality or difference without evidence." is cut with trace diff (OD55).
    O5  Static checks
        flow check checks the complete declared graph, including lazy entries, from each machine's
        compiled data view, without running application callbacks other than entity domain
        recorded positions, which App construction calls once with recording proxies. It reports
        invalid declarations, wiring, ownership and recorded domain rules as errors, including
        violations that untyped input would otherwise bypass. Domain validity follows
        @plan.EntityDomains.O1. A Story hydrate naming a label its source never dehydrates is an
        error. Deny codes come from declared types; missing complete type facts fails instead of
        claiming a complete analysis. A Story is data: check and graph read its steps without
        running a Runtime.

        Errors: unfilled required ports; a raise into a fragment the turn's state change exits;
        two state changers or overlapping keys that state-token pruning cannot separate; an acting
        fragment member outside its fragment; a mapped write or resetOn outside the fragment write
        scope; ReservedStateName; UnknownNotUncertain; a late event with no handler at a strict
        ancestor of its owner, naming the event and the state whose exit makes it late (a fragment
        checked inside its body, plus the host's placement); a late handler with no alternative
        that stays put, at the owner state or its ancestors, strict: a late alternative that
        leaves while later commits of the left state may be queued under serialize or latest is an
        error (it reads only the ending's own operation's lane, a stated limit); failure.NotSent
        unmapped where it can occur (under reject, a key that can hold a kept ending, a lane that
        can be quarantined, serialize, a cancelled operation, a persisted identity, a domain write,
        an input or key that admits a value with no form, or a placement whose subject path is not
        empty, checked at the placement); an adopted ending unhandled in a leaf of its operation's
        arrival set; SharedContract (proposed name) per actor tree, counting fragment placements;
        DuplicatePlacement; VerbMismatch; KeyRequired; UnresolvedHold, with the host's checks for a
        holding placement that does not release on leave; a mapped event used as a schedule's send,
        a raise or a view's send; a contract without key, or a key field that is not a top-level
        input field; KeyFieldOptional; concurrency cancel on a predicting write; Defect or
        Interrupted unmapped in a read operation's tag record; FeedUnhandled, ListenNeedsEvery,
        DuplicateListen, SharedFills, FillsNotPerKey and, not built, UnboundedBridge; ReservedReason
        (proposed), refused at compile wherever a failure tag is declared, and byReason keys that
        are not the commit's own unknown tags or Flow's unknown reasons; GoneWithoutKey,
        GuardedUnbound, GuardedWithoutVersion and GuardedWithoutTarget (proposed names);
        PreviousIsCurrent (proposed); each function passed to an entity domain recorded position
        (inline or by a same-file name) must be one expression of library operators, reads on its
        own parameters, literal constants and a factory's primitive parameters (the allowlist
        grammar), checked with type facts, anything else an error with a fix-it that type-checks or
        an instruction.

        Warnings: likely stuck/unreachable states, unhandled events, unensured send targets, send
        cycles, ambiguous composite ids, duplicated services, untracked entity/list data, sensitive
        SSR fields, entities depending on a lazy byKey loader, and eager or opaque lazy loading;
        send(ref, E.X) when it can reach a state of the target where X is Unhandled (no silencing
        keyword); an overlap only closures separate; a history target over non-resumable fragments
        or a state that owns a call; an exit no reachable transition takes; a hostEvent no host
        raises; a public fragment event unhandled everywhere; a subroutine whose exits are
        unreachable from its default; a state-changing transition listing a reject commit without
        its idle guard; one transition reading, in both memory views, a key it writes;
        KeyOmitsInput; UnusedOperation; UnresolvedHold when the owner exists but is unreachable; a
        one-record domain write whose key fields are not those its predicted record's key reads;
        SharedContract for key [] on a placement whose subject path is not empty; a money-shaped
        write keyed by the Attempt; InputFieldForm; DuplicateSession; ScheduleLeadsToWrite (or
        info, when the schedule is not armed by a write's ending); AsksForever;
        PredictionNotRetained; the Story-derived warnings, read from Story records: a mapper
        reading a key its own turn writes, list repairs and dropped raises, another path's
        declines, denial codes, and rule-20 coverage at a stand-in after a subject change. Info:
        unfilled optional guards; bare writes; each serverMatches field list and each non-literal
        argument to a domain factory; a guard code its port's denials does not name. Each finding
        includes severity, a stable rule reason, its actual path and actionable help; warnings
        never fail the check. Owner plans add findings of their own (entity, lazy, persistence and
        SSR rules), all reported the same way. Failure to load a graph is an invocation failure;
        findings cannot certify arbitrary runtime callbacks or runtime correctness. check --watch
        keeps one compiler session and re-lints changed files. A lint rule with an autofix orders
        the category keys of definitions, fragments, machine bodies, fillings, transitions, commit
        options and operation options, and never reorders entries inside a record.

        Superseded: "without running application callbacks" gains the domain recorded positions'
        recording proxies (merge 5, 6.4) and the compiled data view (C139). "Warnings identify
        likely stuck/unreachable states, unhandled events ..." gains the fragment, server-call,
        tooling, stream and Story-derived findings (fragments row; C18; C65; C81; C101; C125;
        C145; T2; T3); "unhandled events" is sharpened by T3. The late-turn check becomes a strict
        error (C163; C170; C182; C185). ReservedReason, the guarded names, GoneWithoutKey and
        PreviousIsCurrent are added (C177; C193; C209; C215). The key-order lint is added (T4).
    O6  Configuration discovery
        At --project-root (default cwd), flow loads only flow.config.ts, whose default export is
        defineConfig({ apps: [{ app, stories }], stories?, tsconfig? }). stories is a root-relative
        glob discovering exported branded Stories with their own ids; top-level stories are
        Stories with no app (appId null). tsconfig names the program the domain lint reads
        (default tsconfig.json at the project root). The root must contain package.json; discovery
        never searches ancestors or silently chooses another config.

        Missing or unloadable config fails. Empty config, missing apps/Stories, duplicate app ids
        or Story ids within the configuration, foreign setups/machines and structural lookalikes
        reject before acquiring execution resources. Flow and Effect values must come from the
        exact package instances used by the CLI. Focused Stories must belong to their configured
        app.

        Selected config, Story and project entry files must resolve inside the project root after
        symlinks; escaping entries reject before execution. Flow writes no caches or generated
        bundles into the project and removes its external temporary files on success, failure and
        interruption. Loading local TypeScript trusts that code with host authority; it is not a
        sandbox.

        Superseded: "defineConfig({ apps: [{ app, stories }] })" gains top-level stories and
        tsconfig (C81; merge 5, 6.4). "duplicate ... Story ids within an app" reads within the
        configuration (C69). "Revision diff applies the same rule inside its selected revision."
        goes with diff --base (the user's CLI scope of 2026-11-02, OD55).
verification
    V1  Installed CLI tests prove command selection, invalid-input rejection and inert discovery.
    V2  Tests prove direct/CLI Story parity, interruption cleanup and truthful partial traces.
    V3  Tests prove atomic output, no-clobber races, symlink safety and destination preservation.
    V4  Tests prove JSON/text agreement, exclusive result streams, redaction and exit mapping.
    V5  Trace tests prove complete, different, truncated and incompatible query results.
    V6  Check fixtures prove full-graph errors, actionable warnings and absent type-fact failure.
    V7  Tests prove config identity, root containment, ancestor refusal and no project writes.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Exact flag grammar. Atomic publication primitives and flush protocol. Loader, bundler and
        revision-isolation strategy. Console capture bounds and output buffering strategy. Exact
        warning slugs. Static analysis for domains is decided: one source-and-type-facts adapter,
        shared with @plan.Inspection.D2.
